# DENY EVERYTHING, THEN ALLOW SOURCE. Deliberately inverted.
#
# This folder is not a clean project directory - it is a working security tree
# that also holds a 10.9 GB encrypted vault, two Chrome signing keys, three
# built executables and a machine posture baseline. A blocklist would publish
# the next secret someone drops in here. An allowlist ignores it by default.
#
# If you add a new source file type, add an allow rule for it and re-run
# `git status` before committing. If you are tempted to `git add -f`, stop and
# work out why the file is ignored first.

*
!*/

# ---- source that is safe to publish
!.gitignore
!README.md
!LICENSE
!.quickopen.json
!*.ps1
!src/*.py
!src/*.md
!src/*.ps1
!extension/*.js
!extension/*.json
!extension/*.html
!extension/*.css
!nativehost/*.json
!nativehost/*.bat
!ios/**
# ---- docs, packaging, CI, and branding (safe, public)
!docs/*.md
!.github/workflows/*.yml
!packaging/*.iss
!packaging/*.txt
!packaging/branding/*.png
!packaging/branding/*.bmp
!securevault.ico
!securevault.png
# the QuickOpen ROOT cert is public (the trust anchor) — allow just this one
!quickopen-root.crt

# ---- re-denied even though the rules above would otherwise catch them
# private keys: extension.pem and _signing_key.pem sign the Chrome extension.
# Publishing either lets anyone ship a signed impostor of it.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.mobileprovision

# the vault itself, and anything shaped like it
*.dat
*.crx
*.exe
*.exe.bak-*

# build output
build/
dist/
src/build/
src/dist/
src/__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.spec

# scratch / open-in-place plaintext and local env
scratch/
.env
.env.*

# machine-specific state, not source
totp.txt
baseline-*.txt
Backups/
*.log

# generated: contains the DoH bearer token in cleartext
*.mobileconfig
